1
00:00:01,199 --> 00:00:06,716
Welcome. This is a follow-along routine of mouth and throat exercises for snoring.

2
00:00:07,216 --> 00:00:18,408
It comes from a randomized trial where people did these exercises for about eight minutes, three times a day. After three months, the number of snores per hour had roughly halved.

3
00:00:18,908 --> 00:00:28,700
Sit up straight, with your shoulders relaxed. Wash your hands, because one exercise uses a finger. Move slowly and firmly, and stop if anything hurts.

4
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,304
There are five exercises, and I'll count every repetition for you. Let's begin.

5
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:51,061
Exercise one: the tongue slide. Put the tip of your tongue on the roof of your mouth, just behind your top front teeth. Slide it backward along the roof of your mouth, as far as it comfortably goes, then bring it forward again. We’ll do twenty.

6
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:59,046
Rest. Next up: the tongue press.

7
00:02:05,161 --> 00:02:16,201
Exercise two: the tongue press. Suck your whole tongue up against the roof of your mouth, so it lies flat against it, and press firmly. Hold for a moment, then let go. Twenty times.

8
00:03:21,901 --> 00:03:24,437
Rest. Next up: the tongue push-down.

9
00:03:30,301 --> 00:03:43,149
Exercise three: the tongue push-down. Keep the tip of your tongue touching the back of your bottom front teeth. Now push the back of your tongue down, against the floor of your mouth. Then relax. Twenty times.

10
00:04:48,849 --> 00:04:51,627
Rest. Next up: the soft palate lift.

11
00:04:57,249 --> 00:05:08,580
Exercise four: the soft palate lift. Open your mouth and say a short, strong “Ah”. You should feel the back of the roof of your mouth lift up. Say it on every count, twenty times.

12
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:06,600
Rest. Next up: the cheek press.

13
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:27,326
Exercise five: the cheek press. Hook a clean finger inside your cheek, and gently push the cheek outward. Now squeeze your cheek muscle back in, against the finger. Then relax. Ten times on the right side, then ten on the left.

14
00:07:03,027 --> 00:07:04,599
Switch to the left side.

15
00:07:38,227 --> 00:07:47,532
That's the core routine done. If you have a couple more minutes, here are two extras from an earlier trial of these exercises, in people with sleep apnea.

16
00:07:49,632 --> 00:07:56,278
Extra one: the lip press. Close your lips and press them firmly together. Hold the pressure for thirty seconds.

17
00:08:01,579 --> 00:08:02,867
Press, and hold.

18
00:08:31,979 --> 00:08:34,461
Rest. Next up: the balloon breaths.

19
00:08:40,379 --> 00:08:50,367
Extra two, if you have a balloon. Keep it between your lips the whole time. Breathe in deeply through your nose, then blow out hard into the balloon. Five breaths.

20
00:09:36,268 --> 00:09:47,891
One more habit for mealtimes. Chew on both sides of your mouth, taking turns. And when you swallow, keep your teeth together and your tongue up against the roof of your mouth, without squeezing your lips.

21
00:09:50,392 --> 00:10:11,188
Well done, that's one session. Do it three times a day: morning, afternoon and evening. Keep going for at least a few weeks; in the trial, people practiced for three months. Use Snore Log to see if your snoring changes. And if someone notices you stop breathing or gasp at night, or you're very sleepy during the day, see a doctor.
